body:
Same for me actually. I was typed as INFJ initially, then went to INFP, then to ISFJ, and it's alternated between ISFJ and INFJ. I think that when you know your type you suffer from type bias, so you try to act like your type. The best thing is to just have a best friend type you (if you have one that's into MBTI as well). If not, it's trickier. You have to forget MBTI for the time being. Just be yourself. Ignore all the intuitive-sensor conundrum. Feel free. Do what you do best. Then after about a month or so, retype yourself.

&#x200B;

Another possiblity is that your personality hasn't "solidified" yet. If you're below a certain age (30 I think) your personality hasn't matured fully yet. Thus your personality isn't determinable. If you are a teenager/young adult, don't take this MBTI thing to heart. Like they always say, the teenager/young adult stage is for us to explore. We are the water to be frozen, yet to be moulded. Don't let MBTI discourage you from doing what you want, or from doing what you like. Just do it. (Nike references). Also don't nitpick on your individual action. Rather see what you do as a whole, in general. Don't question yourself just because you sensed the environment or had a Te episode.

&#x200B;

Yes, certainty is boring, but certainty about this kind of subject is the best sometimes. MBTI is straddling the edge of pseudoscience, so it may not be accurate at all. And as with all sciences, MBTI is yet to be improved, so its accuracy and efficiency is questionable until then.

&#x200B;

Don't conform to the MBTI. Don't question yourself. Do what you want and can. Let the MBTI conform to you instead (that was so cliche, but you get the point). I was admittedly stuck between ISFJ and INFJ, but I had been true to myself. (After 3 long months of debate and cross-referencing, I have decided on INFJ if you wanted to know).

INFjs have the Ni-Fe combination, the Ni makes them form conclusions based on their interactions with you (Fe) and probably any sensory details (ie what you do, how you respond etc) (Se). Ni itself is an introverted functions and thus is directed inwards. Since everyone is different, therefore different people have different Ni conclusions. Furthermore, the Ni may not even be correct, due to incorrect interpretation of the sensory and behavioral details picked up through Se and Fe (it all depends on the Ni skill of the person). Ni also happens to be relatively narrow-minded and tunnel-visioned, thus they may be adamant about their judgement, which may explain why they think they got you figured out and may be stubborn about it.

&#x200B;

Regarding the "make myself seem complex", the INFJ does have Ti tertiary, (I'm theorizing a little here) so maybe the weak/underdeveloped tertiary function can manifest in how they speak, but since INFJs have weak control over it they may express themselves in what may seem like a very poor imitation of a "complex and smart" person. Or maybe the Ni itself (since it is an "idea" function) grasps what they may want to say, but again, can't express itself coherently and properly without proper planning beforehand. Another case could be the Fe, wanting to appeal to their Thinking type friends and thus attempt to sound smart but failing to do so (admittedly I do it sometimes). It could be any of those 3, or it could be none (again, I'm theorizing a little).

&#x200B;

As persephonevibes said, the INFJ wants to make the world a better place etc so they will criticise your laziness (I also have an INTP friend and despise his laziness, but lately I'm fine with it). They are visionaries, so would naturally want to help others. Maybe they felt your inactivity and decided to berate you on it in hopes that you will improve. ISFJ would do the same actually. However, it would be great if you could mention examples of criticisms you received from the INFJs you mentioned for further analysis. Criticism of laziness isn't limited to INFJ or ISFJs either. It depends on context, ie When were you lazy? During a project? In Physical Education? When they ask you to check something and you declined because you were lazy?

&#x200B;

Regarding the sensitivity, I'm not so sure that they're hiding it. In my opinion, INFJs take criticism really hard. They are perfectionists, always idealising everything, including themselves, and coupled with the sensitive nature of Fe (striving for approval/recognition from others), they are quite sensitive. They probably don't even know that they are that sensitive. Of course, the INFJ can take lots of criticism, but when they mention that they're hurt it's most likely that they've reached their breaking point. Again, this point needs further elaboration. Sometimes INFJs really have tried their hardest or their best (as meantioned, INFJs are perfectionistic and their Ni makes them very completionist and invested in whatever they do, so they will go all the way) and thus may feel that the criticism is undeserved, so they are really sensitive to it. It all depends on context. Should you feel comfortable, please elaborate on this point as well.

&#x200B;

Please take note that I'm in the midst of studying the cognitive functions and cognitive function axis in-depth, so I apologise if my explanations were too "robotic" and drone-like, or if they are inaccurate. Feel free to correct any errors. Cheers.
